1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,307
On the day, the book is down 296 dollars.

7
00:00:28,307 --> 00:00:34,537
State Street SPDR S&P Biotech ETF (XBI) leads, +3.0 percent.

8
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:39,002
Mondelez International (MDLZ) lags at -2.6.

9
00:00:39,002 --> 00:00:44,608
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:44,608 --> 00:00:47,235
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:47,235 --> 00:00:50,900
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,941
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:53,941 --> 00:00:59,472
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:00:59,422 --> 00:01:06,841
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:06,841 --> 00:01:14,733
12 positions, led by Chord Energy, ticker CHRD, Credit Acceptance, ticker CACC, Chevron, ticker CVX.

16
00:01:14,733 --> 00:01:25,466
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:25,428 --> 00:01:29,821
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:29,821 --> 00:01:32,017
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:41,665
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:41,665 --> 00:01:45,273
Inside the book today: 4 positions up, 8 down.

21
00:01:45,273 --> 00:01:47,391
Best, XBI at +3.00 percent.

22
00:01:47,391 --> 00:01:49,038
Worst, CHRD at -2.70.

23
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:53,588
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:01:53,588 --> 00:02:00,098
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.05% per trade over 213 graded positions.

25
00:02:00,098 --> 00:02:06,530
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

26
00:02:06,530 --> 00:02:11,236
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:11,236 --> 00:02:22,532
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 241 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:22,502 --> 00:02:24,953
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:24,953 --> 00:02:35,062
Day 37 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:35,062 --> 00:02:40,371
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 101,947.

31
00:02:40,371 --> 00:02:47,927
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 0.0 thousand back at 101,912.

32
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:55,790
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 2.1 thousand back at 99,855.

33
00:02:55,790 --> 00:02:58,649
The race has no finish line.

34
00:02:58,649 --> 00:03:03,346
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:03,346 --> 00:03:08,656
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:08,656 --> 00:03:10,698
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:10,698 --> 00:03:19,606
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:19,568 --> 00:03:26,903
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:26,903 --> 00:03:29,144
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:29,144 --> 00:03:32,133
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
